    {'path': 'constants_new.py', 'mode': 'edit', 'base_md5': '41e1a07b3528c3fb4871c7f2b1491a76', 'new_md5': 'dc55238f0aa6d72c3f557bd4f1f9cf89', 'zone': None, 'edits': [('(L-456, what a visitor reads: the open address of the copy read goes on\nthe Source line of 62 rows, the Sun\'s and Earth\'s, each re-opened this\nday and given a Read line; PREM\'s three rows re-homed to the model\'s\nopen tabulation; the Roche limit\'s book section corrected, 4.6 to 4.8.)\n"""\n', '(L-456, what a visitor reads: the open address of the copy read goes on\nthe Source line of 63 rows, the Sun\'s and Earth\'s, each re-opened this\nday and given a Read line; PREM\'s three rows re-homed to the model\'s\nopen tabulation; the Roche limit re-homed to Rappaport et al. (2013).)\n"""\n'), ('# Note+: digits.\n# Source: Murray & Dermott, "Solar System Dynamics" (1999), Sec. 4.8\n# Corrected: 2026-10-21 (L-456) -- Sec. 4.6 to 4.8. The book\'s contents put\n# Corrected+: the Roche zone at 4.8; 4.6 is the Darwin-Radau relation. Read\n# Corrected+: on the authors\' book site, https://ssdbook.maths.qmul.ac.uk/toc.html\n# Corrected+: (chapter 4\'s sections in order), 2026-10-21, Claude Opus 5.5, and\n# Corrected+: in the printed contents Gemini 4 (medium) quoted (worksheet_gemini4_medium_l456_sun_20261020.md,\n# Corrected+: S12). The chapter text is not open anywhere this session or\n# Corrected+: Gemini reached (Cambridge sells it), so the formula below has not\n# Corrected+: been read against the book and the row carries no address. It\n# Corrected+: fails The Access Standard until it is re-homed to an open work\n# Corrected+: that states the fluid Roche limit (L-456; the GPT prompt).\n# Derived: Fluid Roche limit formula: d = 2.44 * R * (rho_sun/rho_comet)^(1/3)\n', '# Note+: digits.\n# Source: Rappaport, S., Sanchis-Ojeda, R., Rogers, L. A., Levine, A. and\n# Source+: Winn, J. N. (2013), "The Roche limit for close-orbiting planets:\n# Source+: Minimum density, composition constraints, and application to the\n# Source+: 4.2-hour planet KOI 1843.03", doi:10.1088/2041-8205/773/1/L15,\n# Source+: https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.4080 -- sec. 2, eq. (1): the Roche\n# Source+: limit of a body of incompressible fluid orbiting a star is about\n# Source+: 2.44 R (rho_s/rho_p)^(1/3), R the star\'s radius and rho_s and\n# Source+: rho_p the mean densities of the star and the body.\n# Read: sec. 2, eq. (1), the arXiv PDF of the paper on the Source line,\n# Read+: 2026-10-21, Claude Opus 5.5: "The Roche limiting distance for a body comprised\n# Read+: of an incompressible fluid", a_min = 2.44 R (rho_s/rho_p)^(1/3), "R\n# Read+: is the radius of the parent star". The comet stands for the planet\n# Read+: and the Sun for the star; the formula is the same.\n# Also: Murray & Dermott, "Solar System Dynamics" (1999), Sec. 4.8 ("The\n# Also+: Roche zone"), not read: no open copy exists.\n# Corrected: 2026-10-21 (L-456) -- re-homed from Murray & Dermott, cited\n# Corrected+: here as Sec. 4.6, to an open paper stating the same formula (The\n# Corrected+: Access Standard). The book\'s contents put the Roche zone at\n# Corrected+: 4.8, and 4.6 is the Darwin-Radau relation: read on the authors\'\n# Corrected+: book site, https://ssdbook.maths.qmul.ac.uk/toc.html, and in the\n# Corrected+: printed contents Gemini 4 (medium) quoted (worksheet_gemini4_medium_l456_sun_20261020.md,\n# Corrected+: S12). The comet density the value is worked out from stays\n# Corrected+: unsourced (L-371).\n# Derived: Fluid Roche limit formula: d = 2.44 * R * (rho_sun/rho_comet)^(1/3)\n')]},
    {'path': 'documentation/REVIEW_L456_gemini4_worksheets_20261021.md', 'mode': 'replace', 'base_md5': '2723b650270d85fe6806d25433ed9479', 'new_md5': '58232d9a82460ca0b4aefee7d3470902', 'content': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Review of Gemini 4\'s two address worksheets for L-456 (the Sun\'s 16 works, Earth\'s 23), set against this session\'s own re-opening of every work: what was confirmed, corrected, and not opened, and how each return did against the rules. -->\n# Review: Gemini 4\'s address worksheets for L-456, against a re-opening of every work\n\nBuilt on orrery 6a8d04c2ca0ba94a5fb2f5b70c3ebd82c0d934e3 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and gallery\n30db99fb5ec7c5f996f5adbbf10c23cad1c416a4 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\nWritten October 21, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5, in the build\nsession the coordination brief `BRIEF_L456_addresses_build_20261021.md`\nset up. Skills loaded and matching the manifest: provenance-discipline\n2.32, provenance-cross-check 1.1, interactive-exhibit 1.18,\nledger-and-session-records 1.21, safe-file-editing 1.13.\n\n## How the re-opening was done\n\nEvery work in both prompts was opened again by this session, whatever\nthe worksheet said (provenance-cross-check 1.1, The Slice Relay: a\nreturn is a set of leads). Pages were read through the chat\'s web-fetch\ntool; papers that refuse automated readers were read from Tony\'s own\ncopies in the orrery\'s `papers/` folder through the device link\n(Murray 1989, Shue et al. 1998, Jelinek et al. 2012, the IADC\nguidelines). Where Gemini\'s quote matched what this session read, the\nrow\'s `# Read:` line names both readers. The patch is\n`patch_L456_4_addresses_orrery_20261021.py` (the rows) with\n`patch_L456_5_addresses_website_20261021.py` (the six citations only the\nwebsite writes).\n\n## 1. The Sun\'s worksheet (16 works)\n\n`documentation/worksheets/worksheet_gemini4_medium_l456_sun_20261020.md`,\nGemini 4, medium tier, repository not imported. Its pre-flight PASSED:\nit quoted arXiv:2202.11533\'s first sentence and named the page\ncorrectly (Lamy, Gilardy and Llebaria).\n\n### Confirmed on re-opening, address now on the row (12)\n\n| # | Work | Address written | Where it was read |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S2 | Matese and Whitmire (2011) | https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.4584 (website\'s tide citation) | arXiv PDF, sec. 2.2, "the dominant disk tidal term ... is proportional to \\|sin B cos B\\|" |\n| S3 | Higuchi (2020) | https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.04324 (website\'s tide citation) | arXiv HTML, sec. 5 and sec. 6 item 4, "better than the explanation by Delsemme (1987)" |\n| S4 | Gurnett et al. (2013) | the authors\' copy at space.physics.uiowa.edu (the address Gemini gave, which differs in two places from the one our Access line held) | p. 1489, the 28 July 2012 and 25 August 2012 sentences |\n| S5 | IAU 2015 Resolution B3 | the IAU\'s own PDF | Recommends 3, 1 R_sun^N = 6.957 x 10^8 m |\n| S7 | Liu, Zhu and Hu (2011) | https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201116947, which resolves to A&A\'s free full text | eq. (2) and eq. (8), as the rows quote them |\n| S8 | Murray (1989) | the ADS abstract page (the brief\'s address; ADS refuses this session, so it is the address Tony\'s scan came from) | Tony\'s scan in `papers/`: sect. 7.1, p. 329, the pole of J2000.0 is 12h 51m 26.2755s, +27 deg 7\' 41.704" |\n| S10 | Reid and Brunthaler (2004) | https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0408107 | the arXiv page: title and authors |\n| S11 | NIST SP 811 | NIST\'s chapter 5 page | Table 6: the minute, hour and day, and the minute and second of arc |\n| S13 | NASA Science, "Oort Cloud Facts" | the page | "Scale and Distance", the inner and outer edge sentence |\n| S14 | Portegies Zwart et al. (2021) | https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202040096, which resolves to A&A\'s free full text | sec. 2.2 ("<~20 000 au") and the Fig. 2 and Fig. 3 captions ("about 0.65 pc") |\n| S15 | Stone et al. (2005) | Caltech\'s record of the paper, authors.library.caltech.edu | the abstract sentence, 16 December 2004 at 94.01 AU |\n| S16 | Suess and Nerney (2004) | the ADS abstract page Tony opened on 2026-08-21 | NTRS\'s copy of the abstract, its first sentence |\n\nMurray\'s direction cosines were checked too: the third column of his\neq. (33) gives RA 192.8594812 and Dec 27.1282512 degrees, within\n0.0004 arcseconds of the two pole rows. That was the check the brief\nasked for, against the pole figures cited through Liu, Zhu and Hu.\n\n### Corrected and re-homed (1)\n\n- **S12, Murray and Dermott, the Roche limit.** The row named Sec. 4.6;\n  the book\'s contents put the Roche zone at 4.8 and 4.6 is the\n  Darwin-Radau relation. Read on the authors\' own book site\n  (ssdbook.maths.qmul.ac.uk, chapter 4\'s sections in order) and in the\n  printed contents Gemini quoted. Gemini\'s correction was right. The\n  book has no open copy, so the row was RE-HOMED (The Access Standard)\n  to Rappaport, S., Sanchis-Ojeda, R., Rogers, L. A., Levine, A. and\n  Winn, J. N. (2013), "The Roche limit for close-orbiting planets",\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.4080, sec. 2, eq. (1): "The Roche limiting\n  distance for a body comprised of an incompressible fluid", a_min =\n  2.44 R (rho_s/rho_p)^(1/3), "R is the radius of the parent star".\n  Read 2026-10-21 by this session; the book stays as an Also line at\n  sec. 4.8.\n\n### Did not open (3), all to Tony\'s reading list\n\n- **S1, Delsemme (1987).** ADS refuses automated readers; Semantic\n  Scholar refused this session too. Gemini read it through Semantic\n  Scholar\'s abstract. Address known (ADS).\n- **S6, Archinal et al. (2018), the Sun\'s pole.** Springer, USGS and\n  ResearchGate did not give this session the paper. The PDF in Tony\'s\n  `papers/` folder is the 2019 CORRECTION to the report (Celest. Mech.\n  Dyn. Astron. 131:61), not the report, and does not hold the Sun\'s\n  pole. Gemini quoted Table 1 from ResearchGate. Tony opened the DOI\n  from his phone on 2026-10-21: the paper is not free, and the abstract\n  he pasted names the report but not the Sun\'s pole. RE-HOMED (The\n  Access Standard): NASA NAIF\'s planetary constants kernel\n  pck00011.tpc (N. Bachman, 2022), https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/pck/pck00011.tpc,\n  read 2026-10-21 by this session, names this report as its source and\n  prints BODY10_POLE_RA = 286.13 and BODY10_POLE_DEC = 63.87, the\n  values our pole uses. The website\'s citation now names NAIF\'s file,\n  with the report as the layer below. (The 2019 correction in `papers/`\n  does not touch the Sun.)\n- **S9, the Hipparcos volume, sec. 1.5.3.** The ESA PDF opens but the\n  fetch tool reads only its opening pages, and the sandbox cannot reach\n  cosmos.esa.int. Gemini could not reach p. 91 either and quoted\n  Perryman et al. (1998) instead. Murray (1989) is now read directly\n  and gives the same pole, so this is the third layer, not a gap in the\n  row. Address known.\n\n### Still without a link after this build\n\nNone of the Sun\'s served citations.\n\n### How the Sun\'s return did against the rules\n\nGood. Every row carried a quotation and a locator; it said plainly\nwhere it could not open a page (ADS for S8, the PDF\'s truncation for\nS9) and marked those UNVERIFIED rather than guessing. Its one\ncorrection (S12) was right. Two faults, both in transfer rather than\nreading: six DOIs came wrapped in Google redirect addresses in the\ntable (the bare-URL block was clean), and it named S13\'s author as\nAmanda Barnett where the page today names Alicia Cermak.\n\n## 2. Earth\'s worksheet (23 works)\n\n`worksheet_gemini4_medium_L456_earth_20261021.md`, Gemini 4, medium\ntier, repository not imported (its Canvas names the file\n`..._20261020.md`). It was not in the orrery\'s\n`documentation/worksheets/` when the work began, so the Earth half was\nre-opened from the original prompt\'s rows\n(`documentation/prompts/RELAY_L456_gemini_addresses_earth_20261020.md`)\nwithout it. Tony then sent its share link,\nhttps://gemini.google.com/share/c965fea3cc63, read 2026-10-21 in the\ndesktop app\'s browser; the comparison below is against that page.\nTony saves Gemini\'s exported file into `documentation/worksheets/`;\nthe exported file is the record.\n\n**No Earth row\'s `# Read:` line names Gemini**, even where its quote\nmatches what this session read. A run that did not open its own\npre-flight page has not shown that it reads; a matching quote from it\ncould be a read or a recollection, and the row cannot tell which. The\nSun\'s run, whose pre-flight passed, is named where it matched.\n\n### The return\'s failed pre-flight, first\n\nRecorded from the brief (Claude Fable 5.1, 2026-10-21): asked to open\narXiv:2202.11533 and quote a sentence, Gemini described a Gaia\nlensed-quasar paper by Jalan and others. The page is Lamy, Gilardy and\nLlebaria, "Observations of the Solar F-corona from Space", as the Sun\'s\nrun reported the day before. So that run did not open its pre-flight\npage, and its 23 rows are leads only.\n\nThe page shows the same failure: its pre-flight row names "Gaia GraL:\nGaia Gravitational Lens Systems. VII. X-ray Observations of Lensed\nQuasars; Priyanka Jalan, Jean Surdej, Dominik Sluse, et al.", with\nthe status "Opened and verified in this session".\n\n### Gemini\'s 23 rows against this session\'s reads\n\n- **Matched what this session read (13):** E2, E3, E6, E7, E12, E14,\n  E15, E16, E17, E18, E20, E21, E23. Same quote, same place.\n- **E1, Alken et al.: Gemini wrong.** It puts the pole in Table 3,\n  quoting a ResearchGate PDF, and says Table 4 lists observatories. The\n  journal\'s own page (link.springer.com, through the DOI), read\n  2026-10-21 in the desktop app\'s browser, numbers them the other way:\n  "Table 3 Magnetic observatories contributing data used in the\n  construction of IGRF-13" and "Table 4 Magnetic pole position since\n  1900 determined from IGRF-13". The rows keep Table 4. Its figures,\n  80.65 and -72.68, agree.\n- **E9, IERS TN 36: PARTIAL, about a figure we do not use.** Its note\n  is that Table 1.2 (GRS80) gives a_E = 6378137 m; our row holds\n  6378.1366 km, which is Table 1.1\'s 6378136.6 m, as cited. Its other\n  point agrees with this session\'s read of sec. 2.1.1: the pole offsets\n  are 17.1 and 5.0 mas, and the +/-0.02" there is the FK5 equator\'s\n  accuracy. The pole row\'s Source line already states the offsets.\n- **E5, PREM:** the same three radii, from two other open tabulations\n  (GFZ Potsdam and SOEST Hawaii). This session used the one at P.\n  Shearer\'s UCSD page; any of the three would do.\n- **E8:** the same sentence, from a mirror at juliaastro.org; this\n  session used the IAU\'s own PDF.\n- **E4, Baliukin et al.:** Gemini quotes the abstract from ResearchGate;\n  this session could not open ResearchGate or Wiley. Tony then read\n  it from his phone (Did not open, below), and Gemini\'s quote matches\n  his.\n- **E10, E11, E19, E22: secondary sources, as the brief warned.** E10\n  from a 2010 conference precursor by the same authors; E11 UNVERIFIED\n  (it reached only LASP\'s list and a summary); E19 and E22 from\n  Nishida (2000) and Sibeck and Lin (2014). This session read E10 and\n  E11 in the papers themselves; E19 Tony read in its own abstract; E22 stays on his list. No row\n  was re-homed to a secondary paper.\n- **E13: PARTIAL**, because Li, Tu et al. state the outer belt and not\n  the inner. Our row cites them for the outer belt only, so nothing to\n  change.\n\n### How Earth\'s return did against the rules\n\nPoorly, and visibly so, which is the pre-flight doing its job. The\npre-flight failed, and the run marked it "Opened and verified". Its one\ncorrection (E1\'s table number) was wrong. It leaned on secondary papers\nfor four works, as the brief warned. Its bare-URL block was clean, but\nthe Canvas copy wraps seven DOIs in Google redirect addresses, as the\nSun\'s did. Where it did reach the work (thirteen rows) its quotes are\nverbatim. Taken as leads, it named the open copies for E7 and E12\nthat this session also found.\n\n### Confirmed on re-opening (20)\n\n| # | Work | Address written | Where it was read |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| E1 | Alken et al. (2021) | https://doi.org/10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x, the journal\'s open page | Table 4, row 2020.0, north geomagnetic pole 80.65, -72.68 |\n| E2 | IGRF-13 coefficient file | NOAA NCEI\'s copy of the file | g 1 0, g 1 1, h 1 1 at 2020.0 and their SV |\n| E3 | Baker et al. (2018) | https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-017-0452-7, Springer\'s open full text | sec. 2, r ~ 1.5 R_E |\n| E5 | PREM (Dziewonski and Anderson 1981) | the model\'s open tabulation, Table A.1 of "The PREM Model" at P. Shearer\'s UCSD page (re-homed: the paper is walled) | 1221.5, 3480.0 and 6346.6 km |\n| E6 | Folkner et al. (2014) | JPL\'s own PDF of IPN Progress Report 42-196 | the abstract |\n| E7 | IADC guidelines, Rev. 2 | NASA Orbital Debris Program Office\'s copy, also read in Tony\'s copy | 3.3.1 and 3.3.2 (1), p. 8 |\n| E8 | IAU 2012 Resolution B2 | the IAU\'s PDF of its 2012 resolutions | Recommends 1, the exact au |\n| E9 | IERS Conventions (2010), TN 36 | the IERS Conventions Centre\'s PDF | Tables 1.1 and 1.2; sec. 2.1.1\'s pole offsets |\n| E10 | Jelinek et al. (2012) | https://doi.org/10.1029/2011JA017252 (AGU, free to read; refuses automated readers) | Tony\'s copy: eq. 14, lambda_BS = 1.17, Fig. 7(a) 0.69 R_E |\n| E11 | Li et al. (2025) | NSF\'s public-access copy, par.nsf.gov | sec. 1 para. 1, L = 4 and 5 |\n| E12 | Meredith et al. (2014) | the NERC Open Research Archive\'s copy | introduction para. 1, 1.1 to 2 and 3 to 7 R_E |\n| E13 | Li, Tu et al. (2024) | NSF\'s public-access copy | introduction, 3 to 7 R_E |\n| E14 | NASA Earth Fact Sheet | the page | volumetric mean radius 6371.000 km |\n| E15 | Ishii et al. (2018) | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-24832-y, open | 660 +/- 10 km |\n| E16 | NOAA JetStream | the page | 50 km; 85 to 600 km |\n| E17 | NASA, "Earth\'s Atmospheric Layers" | the NASA image article of that title (January 22, 2013) | 50 km; 600 km |\n| E18 | Shue et al. (1998) | https://doi.org/10.1029/98JA01103 (AGU, free to read; refuses automated readers) | Tony\'s copy: Table 1 "After Fit", a1 to a8; 1.23 R_E |\n| E20 | Slavin et al. (1983) | NTRS\'s record, already on the row; added to the website\'s magnetotail citation | the abstract: out to X = -220 Earth radii; five authors as the row names them |\n| E21 | Sibeck and Lin (2014) | NTRS\'s PDF (website\'s magnetotail citation) | the abstract: cross-sections "elongated in the direction parallel to the component of the IMF in the plane perpendicular to the Sun-Earth line" |\n| E23 | JPL Horizons manual | the manual\'s page | "International Celestial Reference Frame (ICRF)": at most 0.02 arcseconds |\n\nEighteen of these gained an address on orrery rows (E1 to E3, E5 to\nE18, and E23). E20\'s row already carried its NTRS address; E20 and E21\ngain theirs in the website\'s own magnetotail citation.\n\nOne note from the reading. E17: the page our row means is NASA\'s "Earth\'s Atmospheric Layers"\nimage article; NASA Science\'s "multi-layered cake" page gives the\nthermosphere to 700 km and is a different page.\n\n### Did not open (1), to Tony\'s reading list; two he read\n\n- **E4, Baliukin et al. (2019): read by Tony.** Wiley and HAL refuse\n  automated readers. Tony opened the DOI from his phone on 2026-10-21\n  (the paper is free) and pasted the abstract: "The geocorona was found\n  to extend at least up to 100 Earth radii (RE) with an intensity of 5\n  Rayleigh". The row carries the DOI\'s address and names Tony.\n- **E19, Slavin et al. (1985): read by Tony.** Wiley refuses automated\n  readers, and the NTRS hit for it is a different paper (a 1985\n  Chapman Conference paper by the same group). Tony opened the DOI from\n  his phone on 2026-10-21 and pasted the abstract, which is free though\n  the paper is not: "On the basis of 756 magnetopause crossings a\n  low-latitude magnetotail diameter of 60+/-5 RE at |X| = 130 - 225 RE\n  is determined" and "Flaring ceases on average at |X| = 120 +/- 10\n  RE". Both rows and the website\'s magnetotail citation now carry the\n  DOI\'s address, and the rows\' Read lines name Tony.\n- **E22, Maezawa et al. (1997).** ScienceDirect refuses automated\n  readers. Address known (DOI). Cited only in the website\'s own\n  magnetotail words.\n\n### Secondary papers (the brief\'s E10, E11, E19, E22)\n\nThe brief warned that Gemini leaned on secondary papers for these four.\nThis session used none: E10 was read in the paper itself (Tony\'s copy),\nE11 in the paper itself (NSF\'s copy of the same article), E19 in its\nown abstract (Tony\'s read), and E22 was not opened and goes to Tony. Nothing was re-homed to a\nsecondary paper.\n\n## 3. What is left without a link, by name\n\nSun (0).\nEarth (0). Maezawa et al. (1997) sits inside\nthe website\'s magnetotail citation, which now links its other three\nworks.\n\nSession record written October 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n'},
    {'path': 'documentation/READING_L456_tony_20261021.md', 'mode': 'replace', 'base_md5': '66e6d26e3df9aafbdf8f3dd8ce9670be', 'new_md5': 'b6aeaddf83bb9c18ccc78497682411de', 'content': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Tony\'s reading list for L-456: the works whose address is known but which refused this session\'s readers. Open each in your browser, save the PDF into the orrery\'s papers/ folder, and the next session reads it there and writes the row. -->\n# Your reading list: papers that refused the session (L-456)\n\nBuilt on orrery 6a8d04c2ca0ba94a5fb2f5b70c3ebd82c0d934e3 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and gallery\n30db99fb5ec7c5f996f5adbbf10c23cad1c416a4 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\nWritten October 21, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n\n## What to do\n\nFor each paper below:\n\n1. Open the address in your browser.\n2. Save the PDF into the orrery\'s `papers/` folder. Any file name is\n   fine; the folder is not committed.\n3. If no PDF is offered, say so in the "Your note" line. A screenshot\n   of the abstract saved in `papers/` works too.\n\nYou do not need to check the numbers. The next session reads each file\nthrough the device link and writes the row\'s `# Read:` line with your\nname and date as the one who opened it. "What to look for" is there so\nyou know you have the right paper.\n\n## The six (three done)\n\n**1. Delsemme (1987), the comets and the galactic tide (Sun room).**\n- Open: https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1987A&A...187..913D/abstract\n  (on the page, "Full Text Sources", then the ADS scan.)\n- Look for: title "Galactic tides affect the Oort cloud: an\n  observational confirmation", A&A 187, 913.\n- The row expects: the aphelia of 152 long-period comets avoid both\n  galactic polar caps and a strip along the galactic equator.\n- Your note:\n\n**2. Archinal et al. (2018), the Sun\'s north pole (Sun room).**\n- Open: https://doi.org/10.1007/s10569-017-9805-5\n- Look for: "Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic\n  Coordinates and Rotational Elements: 2015", Celest. Mech. Dyn. Astron.\n  130:22. Note: the file already in `papers/` is the 2019 CORRECTION to\n  this report, not the report.\n- The row expects: Table 1, the Sun, alpha0 = 286.13, delta0 = 63.87.\n- Your note: DONE 2026-10-21, from your phone. Not free; the abstract\n  names the report but not the pole. The citation was re-homed to NASA\n  NAIF\'s open file of the report\'s values. Nothing to save.\n\n**3. The Hipparcos catalogue, volume 1, section 1.5.3 (Sun room).**\n- Open: https://www.cosmos.esa.int/documents/532822/552851/vol1_all.pdf\n  (a large file; page 91 of the book is about page 135 of the PDF.)\n- Look for: section 1.5.3, "Coordinate Transformations".\n- The row expects: the north galactic pole at about RA 192.85948 deg,\n  Dec +27.12825 deg (the same pole Murray 1989 gives, which is now\n  read).\n- Your note:\n\n**4. Baliukin et al. (2019), the hydrogen geocorona (Earth room).**\n- Open: https://doi.org/10.1029/2018JA026136\n- Look for: "SWAN/SOHO Lyman-alpha mapping: the hydrogen geocorona\n  extends well beyond the Moon", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 124.\n- The row expects: in the abstract, the geocorona "found to extend at\n  least up to 100 Earth Radii".\n- Also: does the page let you read the full paper free? Say yes or no.\n- Your note: DONE 2026-10-21, from your phone. The paper is free; you\n  pasted the abstract. Save the PDF into `papers/` when you are at your\n  machine (you said you would); the row already names you.\n\n**5. Slavin et al. (1985), the distant magnetotail (Earth room).**\n- Open: https://doi.org/10.1029/JA090iA11p10875\n- Look for: "An ISEE 3 study of average and substorm conditions in the\n  distant magnetotail", J. Geophys. Res. 90(A11).\n- The row expects: in the abstract, flaring ceases on average at\n  |X| = 120 +/- 10 Earth radii; from 756 magnetopause crossings, a tail\n  diameter of 60 +/- 5 Earth radii at |X| = 130 to 225.\n- Also: full paper free to read? Yes or no.\n- Your note: DONE 2026-10-21, from your phone. The paper is not free;\n  the abstract is, and you pasted it. Both rows now carry the DOI\'s\n  address with you as the reader. Nothing to save.\n\n**6. Maezawa et al. (1997), the distant tail\'s shape (Earth room).**\n- Open: https://doi.org/10.1016/S0273-1177(97)00503-6\n- Look for: "Structure of the distant magnetotail and its dependence on\n  the IMF By component: GEOTAIL observations", Adv. Space Res. 20(4-5).\n- The row expects: in the abstract, GEOTAIL found the distant tail\n  (beyond about 150 Earth radii) about the same size in both directions\n  under average conditions.\n- Your note:\n\n## Not on this list\n\n- The Roche limit\'s book (Murray and Dermott). No free copy exists; the\n  row now cites an open paper with the same formula (Rappaport et al.\n  2013, arXiv:1307.4080).\n\nSession record written October 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n'},
    {'path': 'documentation/HANDOFF_L456_addresses_build_20261021.md', 'mode': 'replace', 'base_md5': 'eb9143eb11ddc1e69b913976a397eaee', 'new_md5': '9321070a17dafe5f3cb442b09db01b05', 'content': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Handoff from the build session of 2026-10-21: the design talks\' close patch, then L-456\'s addresses for the Sun\'s and Earth\'s 39 works, re-opened and written onto their rows and the website\'s own citations; Tony\'s reading list for what did not open, none of it missing a link. -->\n# Handoff: the talks\' close and L-456\'s 39 addresses (October 21, 2026)\n\nBuilt on orrery 6a8d04c2ca0ba94a5fb2f5b70c3ebd82c0d934e3 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and gallery\n30db99fb5ec7c5f996f5adbbf10c23cad1c416a4 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io. Neither\nhad moved when the patches were written. Nothing is pushed by this\nsession; the patches are in your folders.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\n- Type: BUILD.\n- Follows `BRIEF_L456_addresses_build_20261021.md` (Claude Fable 5.1),\n  which patch 3 saves into `documentation/`. Companion records: the\n  review, `REVIEW_L456_gemini4_worksheets_20261021.md`, and your reading\n  list, `READING_L456_tony_20261021.md`.\n- Skills loaded, each matching the manifest at 6a8d04c:\n  ledger-and-session-records 1.21, safe-file-editing 1.13,\n  provenance-discipline 2.32, provenance-cross-check 1.1, and\n  interactive-exhibit 1.18. That last one discharges the obligation\n  v3.106 carried: the loaded copy reads 1.18.\n\n## 1. Run order (in this order)\n\nWhat already ran, from Tony\'s run record of 12:17: patch 3, then an\nEARLIER copy of patch 4 (the Roche limit not yet re-homed, a GPT prompt\nwritten), then the orrery maintenance run, all passing. The orrery was\nNOT committed or pushed (its HEAD was still 6a8d04c at 12:38). Then,\non the website, an earlier copy of patch 5 (seven edits, the Sun\'s pole\nnot yet re-homed), the Daily Run, commit and push at gallery 53f17b6.\nThat Daily Run pulled the orrery\'s PUSHED export, which is still the\nold one, so the website served the old, unlinked citations (what\nTony\'s phone showed at 12:34).\n\n1. **Orrery folder:** run `patch_L456_4b_roche_rehome_20261021.py`.\n   It brings the files the earlier patch 4 wrote to the final\n   version: the Roche limit re-homed to Rappaport et al. (2013), the\n   records\' counts, and the GPT prompt removed (none is needed). Move\n   it into `documentation/`. Run the orrery maintenance run: expect 31\n   of 31 gating and the gate line 0 TIER-1. Then commit AND push. The\n   push is the step that puts the links on the website.\n2. **Gallery folder:** run `patch_L456_6_sun_pole_website_20261021.py`,\n   the Sun\'s pole only, and move it into `documentation/`. Then the\n   Daily Run: its pull now brings the orrery\'s new export; the Config\n   mirror writes the addresses into `data/objects_config.json`; the\n   cache builder serves them. Expect 31 of 31 gating and "Source list"\n   PASS. Commit and push, then the live run.\n3. **Your phone:** the Sun room and the Earth room, the i panel. Tap a\n   link in each. The Earth inner core\'s Dziewonski and Anderson now\n   opens (PREM\'s open tabulation), and so does the Sun\'s Roche limit\n   (Rappaport et al. 2013).\n\n## 2. What was built (verified on copies, not claimed)\n\n**Patch 3, the design talks\' close** (orrery; ledger, Where We Are, and\nthe brief saved). The talks\' handoff, section 3, line by line, on\nL-401, L-411, L-412, L-363, L-434, L-239, L-224, L-356, L-375, L-454\nand L-413; your phone look and the Gemini answers on L-456, with the\nMurray scan and the Roche section number noted for the build; L-460\nnew, the drawer order (section 5 of the brief); Where We Are by\nsection. Tested on a copy with a Windows-line-endings page and a note\nof yours below the run-record marker: it applied, wrote LF, left the\nnote, refused a second run, and the ledger indexer and document\nindexer passed after it. The page stands at 130 lines above the marker,\nthe cap.\n\n**Patch 4, the addresses on the orrery\'s rows.** 75 anchored edits to\n`constants_new.py` (the rows\' changes and the module\'s stamp), checked to\nreproduce the built file exactly. 63\nrows\' Source lines gain the address of the copy this session opened,\nthe Roche limit\'s re-homed to an open paper. Every\nrow gets a `# Read:` line naming this session and date, and Gemini\nwhere its quote matched (the Sun\'s half). It also adds the review, your\nreading list and this handoff to `documentation/`, and\nputs the close on L-456 and L-412 and Where We Are. On a copy with patch\n3 applied and indexed: the constants export ran, 198 rows; the orrery\nmaintenance run, beside a copy of the website, passed every checker\nexcept the five that need Tk, which this sandbox lacks (Period fetch\nsuite, Reset completeness, Earth pole of date, Position digits, Orbit\nmarkers; each fails the same way on the unedited repository here).\nThe gate line read 0 TIER-1, whole tree 280, and no file\'s count rose:\nthe patch predicts no change.\n\n**Patch 4b** (written after Tony had run an earlier patch 4): the\nRoche limit\'s re-home and the records\' counts, on top of that run.\n\n**Patch 5, the website\'s own seven citations.** In\n`data/objects_config.json`, the citations the website writes itself,\nnot the mirror: the galactic pole (twice), the galactic tide, Sgr A*\'s\nplace, the magnetotail, Earth\'s fallback pole, and the Sun\'s pole\n(re-homed to NASA NAIF\'s planetary constants file, which carries the\nIAU report\'s values). Eight anchored edits;\nthe file stays valid JSON. On a copy with the new export mirrored in:\nevery website checker passed except Cache in step, which fails until\nthe cache builder runs (the Daily Run runs it before that check).\n\n**The counts**, distinct served citation texts with a link, worked out\non the copies from `data/objects_config.json`:\n\n| Room | Before (gallery 30db99f) | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sun | 23 of 40 | 40 of 40 |\n| Earth | 33 of 73 | 73 of 73 |\n\nThe website\'s Source list check counts differently (by drawer row):\nthe Sun 24 of 43 before, 42 of 43 after. Its Earth count stays at 9 of\n29, because it reads a saved Earth scene that the Daily Run does not\nremake (section 4).\n\n## 3. Still without an address, by name\n\n- **The Sun:** none. The Roche limit is re-homed: Murray and Dermott\n  has no open copy, and Rappaport, Sanchis-Ojeda, Rogers, Levine and\n  Winn (2013), arXiv:1307.4080, give the same fluid formula in their\n  eq. (1), 2.44 R (rho_s/rho_p)^(1/3); the book stays on the row as an\n  Also line. The Sun\'s pole is linked now: Archinal et al. (2018) is\n  not free, so the citation names NASA NAIF\'s open file of the report\'s\n  values, with the report as the layer below.\n- **Earth:** none. Slavin et al. (1985) and Baliukin et al. (2019)\n  are linked now: you read their abstracts from your phone on\n  2026-10-21, and their rows name you as the reader. Baliukin\'s paper\n  is free; Slavin\'s is not, its abstract is.\n- On your reading list but not missing a link: Delsemme (1987), inside\n  the galactic tide\'s citation; the Hipparcos volume, the third layer\n  under the galactic pole; and Maezawa et al. (1997), inside the\n  magnetotail citation whose other three works are linked.\n\n## 4. Found while building (recorded on L-456, not chased)\n\n- The Source list check counts the Earth room from\n  `documentation/payload_earth_scene.json`, which only\n  `tools/record_earth_scene.py` remakes. The Daily Run does not run\n  it, so the check\'s Earth number will not move with this build. The\n  live Earth room reads the cache and is not affected.\n- The Earth room\'s fallback-pole citation, written by the website,\n  says "cited here until 2026-09-26": project words a visitor reads.\n\n## 5. Not done this session, and why\n\n- **Job 4, the drawer order (L-460, interactive-exhibit 1.19).** The\n  addresses filled the session, as the brief allowed. Next build.\n- **Job 5, the Sun\'s fourteen pairs (L-191).** Not reached.\n- **Earth\'s Gemini worksheet** arrived by share link after the rows\n  were built. The review compares its 23 rows (section 2). No Earth row\n  names Gemini as a reader: its run failed its pre-flight, so a\n  matching quote from it cannot be told from a recollection. Its one\n  correction, the pole\'s Table 3, was wrong; the journal\'s own page\n  numbers the pole table 4.\n\n## 6. For the next session\n\n1. Confirm both repositories\' HEADs and read Tony\'s newest run record.\n2. If Tony has saved papers from the reading list into `papers/`, read\n   each through the device link and write its row\'s `# Read:` line with\n   his name as the one who opened it; addresses for the website\'s own\n   words go in a gallery patch, as patch 5 did.\n3. Then L-460 (the drawer order, with interactive-exhibit 1.19) and\n   L-191 (the fourteen pairs), the Sun\'s slice as the road has it.\n\n## 7. Tony-actions\n\n- (do) The run order in section 1.\n- (do) Your reading list: three papers into `papers/`, none of them\n  missing a link (Delsemme, the Hipparcos volume, Maezawa).\n- (do) Save Gemini\'s exported Earth file into\n  `documentation/worksheets/` as\n  `worksheet_gemini4_medium_L456_earth_20261021.md` (patch 3 reminds\n  you).\n- (do) Your phone look, section 1 step 4.\n\n## Where this leaves us\n\n- Every Sun and Earth source has a link; no GPT search was needed.\n- The Oct 20 talks\' rulings are on the ledger with this morning\'s patch.\n- Next: the drawer order, then the Sun\'s fourteen pairs.\n\nSession written October 21, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n'},
